//! Verbora vs. a real, pinned third-party Rust competitor — phonetic
//! encoding (Soundex, Metaphone, Double Metaphone, Daitch-Mokotoff Soundex).
//!
//! Reads the same `benches/data/names.json` this crate's own
//! `tests/phonetics_correctness.rs` reads, generated by
//! `tools/bench-data/generate.py` — a curated list of ~650 real,
//! recognizable personal names spanning many languages and writing systems
//! (romanized/transliterated to plain ASCII letters; see that generator's
//! own comment for why not a synthetic random draw, and the ASCII-only note
//! below for why not the original scripts). See
//! `docs/COMPETITIVE_BENCHMARKS.md` §1.6 for why `rphonetic` 3.0.6 was
//! selected (the only actively-maintained Rust crate covering all four of
//! Verbora's phonetic families from the canonical Apache commons-codec
//! reference) and `../../README.md` for why this crate lives outside the
//! main workspace.
//!
//! # This is a throughput-only comparison, not a byte-exact one
//!
//! Every row here is marked `Partial` in the matrix, never `Yes`: Verbora
//! transcribes the reference's own variants (condense-before-drop Soundex, a
//! Metaphone/Double Metaphone with a documented stage-ordering quirk and a
//! family of the reference-truthiness accidents, a single-branch
//! Daitch-Mokotoff), while rphonetic implements the textbook / Apache
//! commons-codec originals. The two sides are expected to disagree on the
//! literal encoded string for real input — that is documented, not a bug in
//! this bench — so **no benchmark here asserts output equality between
//! Verbora and rphonetic**, only that both are doing the same *shape* of
//! work on the same input. What genuine correctness properties there are to
//! check (crash-safety on the whole shared dataset, the `max_code_length`
//! reconfiguration below actually taking effect, output-shape parity) are
//! checked once, outside the timed code, in
//! `tests/phonetics_correctness.rs`, per this project's `CORRECTNESS BEFORE
//! PERFORMANCE` rule.
//!
//! # Fairness reconfiguration: `max_code_length`
//!
//! rphonetic's `Metaphone`/`DoubleMetaphone` default to a maximum code
//! length of **4** (`Metaphone::default()`); Verbora's default is **32**
//! (`Metaphone::new().process(...)`, i.e. `process_with(_, None)`, which
//! resolves to 32 — see `crates/verbora-phonetics/src/metaphone.rs`). Both
//! encoders below are therefore constructed with `new(Some(32))`, not
//! `default()` — using the default would have rphonetic doing strictly less
//! work (truncating four times earlier) and make it look faster than it
//! fairly is. `tests/phonetics_correctness.rs` proves this reconfiguration
//! actually changes the observed output length rather than silently no-op'ing.
//!
//! # Daitch-Mokotoff: `encode()`, never `soundex()`
//!
//! rphonetic's `DaitchMokotoffSoundex` has two public methods: `.soundex()`,
//! the genuine multi-branch D-M algorithm that can return up to 8
//! pipe-separated codes, and `.encode()` (the `Encoder` trait method,
//! single-branch, exactly one code). Verbora's `SoundExDM::process` returns
//! one `String`. Only `.encode()` matches that output shape, so only
//! `.encode()` is benchmarked here — using `.soundex()` would silently
//! compare a materially larger amount of work.
//!
//! # No Cologne row
//!
//! rphonetic also implements Cologne phonetics, but the reference exports
//! exactly four phonetic classes and no Cologne — so there is no Verbora
//! capability for it to compete against, and the matrix produces no Cologne
//! row anywhere (confirmed by reading the reference's phonetics modules directly, not
//! assumed from the class list).
//!
//! # Why `names.json` is ASCII-letter only
//!
//! rphonetic's `Soundex::encode` looks up `self.mapping[ch as usize - 65]`
//! against a fixed 26-entry array with no bounds check — a non-ASCII letter
//! (e.g. an accented Latin character, Cyrillic, CJK) is a genuine
//! **out-of-bounds panic** there, not merely an unfair comparison. Verbora's
//! own encoders handle arbitrary Unicode (promoting to UTF-16 code units;
//! see `crates/verbora-phonetics/src/units.rs`), so the ASCII restriction is
//! rphonetic's, not Verbora's — narrowing the shared input domain to
//! ASCII-letter names is what keeps this specific comparison both fair and
//! crash-safe, the same policy `../distance.rs` already applies to its own
//! `strsim`/`rapidfuzz` rows for a related (BMP-vs-astral-plane) reason.
//!
//! # Scales
//!
//! Per `Fase 6 Benchmark.md`'s own PHONETICS BENCHMARKS section (single
//! encode / 10K / 100K / 1M names, throughput): this pass covers **single
//! encode, 10,000, and 100,000** names. 100,000 matches the top scale
//! `crates/verbora-phonetics/benches/phonetics.rs`'s own `parallel_batch`
//! group already uses for the same reason it gives ("a realistic large
//! dictionary"); a 1,000,000-name scale is left to a future pass rather than
//! benchmarked here. The base ~650-name list is cycled up to each target
//! size (`Iterator::cycle`), exactly as that in-crate `parallel_batch` group
//! already cycles its own word list — every scale therefore draws from the
//! same realistic name distribution, just repeated further. "Single encode"
//! uses one fixed representative surname (`SINGLE_NAME`) rather than a
//! rotating sample, matching `../distance.rs`'s own convention of one fixed
//! input per size rather than a different input per iteration (Criterion
//! measures the same input across many repeated iterations for a stable
//! statistic; that is the same methodology as every other single-op
//! benchmark in this codebase).
//!
//! Every call's input and output is wrapped in `black_box`, per this
//! project's `BLACK-BOX EVERYTHING` rule.
//!
//! # Beider-Morse: a coverage gap this file left open until now
//!
//! `crates/verbora-phonetics/src/beider_morse/mod.rs` (a Verbora-native
//! extension added this same round, no reference counterpart — the reference
//! has no Beider-Morse implementation) names `rphonetic` by name in its own
//! doc comment as the design-time cross-checking oracle its correctness was
//! verified against, and explicitly points here for where that crate *is* a
//! real dependency. `rphonetic`'s `embedded_bm` Cargo feature was never
//! enabled and no bench group existed until this pass — a real,
//! previously-undiscovered gap, not a deliberate exclusion.
//!
//! `bench_beider_morse` is `Partial`, same as every other row in this file:
//! `rphonetic`'s `embedded_bm` feature embeds **only the `"any"`/`"common"`
//! rule files**, not the full per-language corpus each `NameType` otherwise
//! has (confirmed by reading `ConfigFiles`'s own doc comment in
//! `rphonetic-3.0.6/src/beider_morse/mod.rs`) — so its `Default`-constructed
//! encoder can never resolve a *specific* guessed language, only ever the
//! `"any"` fallback file, regardless of input. Verbora's own `.encode()`
//! guesses freely across its full embedded 18/10/5-language corpus per
//! `NameType`. Both sides are benchmarked through their own default,
//! caller-facing `.encode()` entry point rather than trying to force an
//! artificially-matched code path — the same "same shape of work, not a
//! byte-exact comparison" policy this file's own module doc comment
//! already states for Soundex/Metaphone/Double Metaphone/Daitch-Mokotoff,
//! not a new exception invented for this one row.
//!
//! # Beider-Morse: its own, smaller scales
//!
//! A single Beider-Morse `.encode()` call costs roughly three orders of
//! magnitude more than this file's other four encoders (tens to low
//! hundreds of microseconds per name — the Rules-pass sweep plus a
//! candidate-generation cross-product, versus a handful of nanoseconds for
//! a Soundex/Metaphone character scan) — `batches`' own 100,000-name top
//! scale would take an unreasonably long time to complete here. `bench_beider_morse`
//! therefore uses its own, independently-chosen scales (1, 100, 1,000)
//! rather than reusing `batches()`.

use criterion::{BenchmarkId, Criterion, Throughput, black_box, criterion_group, criterion_main};

use rphonetic::{
    BeiderMorseBuilder, ConfigFiles, DaitchMokotoffSoundex, DoubleMetaphone as RDoubleMetaphone,
    Encoder, Metaphone as RMetaphone, Soundex as RSoundex,
};
use verbora_phonetics::{
    BeiderMorse, DoubleMetaphone, Metaphone, NameType, RuleType, SoundEx, SoundExDM,
};

/// One fixed, realistic, moderately complex surname for the "single encode"
/// scale — see the module doc comment's "Scales" section for why a fixed
/// input rather than a rotating sample.
const SINGLE_NAME: &str = "Kowalski";

/// Reads the shared name list, failing loudly if it has not been generated.
fn load_names() -> Vec<String> {
    let path = std::path::Path::new(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"))
        .ancestors()
        .nth(3)
        .expect("repo root is 3 levels up from rust-competitors/")
        .join("benches/data/names.json");
    let body = std::fs::read_to_string(&path).unwrap_or_else(|e| {
        panic!(
            "cannot read {}: {e}\nGenerate it with: python3 tools/bench-data/generate.py",
            path.display()
        )
    });
    let json: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(&body).expect("valid bench data");
    json["names"]
        .as_array()
        .expect("names array")
        .iter()
        .map(|w| w.as_str().expect("name is a string").to_owned())
        .collect()
}

/// The three scales this file benchmarks, built once and shared by every
/// group below so all four algorithms run on byte-identical batches at each
/// size.
fn batches(names: &[String]) -> Vec<(usize, Vec<&str>)> {
    const SIZES: [usize; 3] = [1, 10_000, 100_000];
    SIZES
        .iter()
        .map(|&n| {
            let batch = if n == 1 {
                vec![SINGLE_NAME]
            } else {
                names.iter().map(String::as_str).cycle().take(n).collect()
            };
            (n, batch)
        })
        .collect()
}

fn bench_soundex(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let names = load_names();
    let scales = batches(&names);

    let verbora = SoundEx::new();
    let rphonetic = RSoundex::default();

    let mut g = c.benchmark_group("soundex");
    for (n, batch) in &scales {
        g.throughput(Throughput::Elements(*n as u64));
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verbora", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += verbora.process(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("rphonetic", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += rphonetic.encode(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
    }
    g.finish();
}

fn bench_metaphone(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let names = load_names();
    let scales = batches(&names);

    let verbora = Metaphone::new();
    // `Some(32)`, not `default()` (which is `Some(4)`) — see the module doc
    // comment's "Fairness reconfiguration" section.
    let rphonetic = RMetaphone::new(Some(32));

    let mut g = c.benchmark_group("metaphone");
    for (n, batch) in &scales {
        g.throughput(Throughput::Elements(*n as u64));
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verbora", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += verbora.process(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("rphonetic", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += rphonetic.encode(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
    }
    g.finish();
}

fn bench_double_metaphone(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let names = load_names();
    let scales = batches(&names);

    let verbora = DoubleMetaphone::new();
    let rphonetic = RDoubleMetaphone::new(Some(32));

    let mut g = c.benchmark_group("double_metaphone");
    for (n, batch) in &scales {
        g.throughput(Throughput::Elements(*n as u64));
        // Both sides compute *both* keys (primary + alternate) per call —
        // Verbora's `process` always returns the pair, so the fair rphonetic
        // counterpart is `.double_metaphone(...)` (both codes), not the
        // `Encoder::encode` trait method (primary only, half the work).
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verbora", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    let (primary, alternate) = verbora.process(black_box(w));
                    total += primary.len() + alternate.len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("rphonetic", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    let result = rphonetic.double_metaphone(black_box(w));
                    total += result.primary().len() + result.alternate().len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
    }
    g.finish();
}

fn bench_dm_soundex(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let names = load_names();
    let scales = batches(&names);

    let verbora = SoundExDM::new();
    let rphonetic = DaitchMokotoffSoundex::default();

    let mut g = c.benchmark_group("dm_soundex");
    for (n, batch) in &scales {
        g.throughput(Throughput::Elements(*n as u64));
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verbora", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += verbora.process(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
        // `.encode()`, never `.soundex()` — see the module doc comment's
        // "Daitch-Mokotoff: encode(), never soundex()" section.
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("rphonetic", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += rphonetic.encode(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
    }
    g.finish();
}

/// Beider-Morse's own, smaller scales — see the module doc comment's
/// "Beider-Morse: its own, smaller scales" section for why this doesn't
/// reuse `batches()`.
fn beider_morse_batches(names: &[String]) -> Vec<(usize, Vec<&str>)> {
    const SIZES: [usize; 3] = [1, 100, 1_000];
    SIZES
        .iter()
        .map(|&n| {
            let batch = if n == 1 {
                vec![SINGLE_NAME]
            } else {
                names.iter().map(String::as_str).cycle().take(n).collect()
            };
            (n, batch)
        })
        .collect()
}

fn bench_beider_morse(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let names = load_names();
    let scales = beider_morse_batches(&names);

    let verbora = BeiderMorse::new(NameType::Generic, RuleType::Approx);
    // `embedded_bm`-only `Default`: "any"/"common" rules alone -- see the
    // module doc comment's "Beider-Morse: a coverage gap" section for why
    // this can never resolve a specific guessed language.
    let config_files = ConfigFiles::default();
    let rphonetic = BeiderMorseBuilder::new(&config_files).build();

    let mut g = c.benchmark_group("beider_morse");
    for (n, batch) in &scales {
        g.throughput(Throughput::Elements(*n as u64));
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("verbora", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += verbora.encode(black_box(w)).spellings.len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
        g.bench_with_input(BenchmarkId::new("rphonetic", n), batch, |b, batch| {
            b.iter(|| {
                let mut total = 0usize;
                for w in black_box(batch.as_slice()) {
                    total += rphonetic.encode(black_box(w)).len();
                }
                black_box(total)
            });
        });
    }
    g.finish();
}

criterion_group!(
    benches,
    bench_soundex,
    bench_metaphone,
    bench_double_metaphone,
    bench_dm_soundex,
    bench_beider_morse,
);
criterion_main!(benches);
